<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stability" target="global_inflation_and_rat::inflation_expectations" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.9</data>
  <data key="d36">Interruptions or volatility in Russian/Ukraine-related gas and oil flows push spot energy prices up and increase price volatility, which market participants and consumers incorporate into near-term inflation forecasts.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_inflation_and_rat</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stability" target="global_inflation_and_rat::long_term_gov_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained energy-supply disruptions raise expected future inflation and sovereign financing needs (subsidies, defense/reconstruction), increasing term premia and yields on long-maturity government bonds.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_inflation_and_rat</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stability" target="iran_gulf_flashpoint::oil_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.9</data>
  <data key="d36">Deterioration in energy supply stability caused by the Russia–Ukraine conflict tightens physical flows and risk premia in global markets, directly pushing benchmark oil prices higher.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_flashpoint</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stability" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::ai_capital_allocation" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">More stable and predictable energy supplies reduce operational risk and projected power costs for large AI compute projects, increasing investor willingness to commit capital to AI infrastructure and firms, whereas instability drives capital away or into less energy‑intensive geographies.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stability" target="us_political_shift::policy_uncertainty" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Greater stability in energy supplies (fewer disruptions from the conflict) reduces the need for emergency legislation, tariff or sanction shifts and ad hoc interventions, thereby lowering near-term policy unpredictability.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_political_shift</data>
</edge>
